Z||000000||Stungbymystang||11-26-2001||12:48 PM||||Ok, after careful consideration, I have decided to purchase a Fiero.<P>I know nothing about them other than I like the last couple of years they were made best!!! That's the one I want! <P>Now, what exactly am I looking for? Engine? Body, etc.?<P>About how much will a decent one cost? Are parts very hard to find, because I'm sure if I buy one, it will need some work done!<P>I know how it is with newbies and thier redundant questions, so I appologize in advance, but I just made up my mind that after the new year I am purchasing one. I am looking in the paper as I write this and haven't seen one for sale yet, maybe I'll have better luck tommorrow.<P>But how much should I be willing to spend, so I know I'm not being ripped a new one!<P>Thanks guys!!!!<P>Also, what are the major problem areas?<P>------------------<BR>Check Her Out <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/xvwannabemevx/home.html" TARGET=_blank>Jade Not Your Average 6</A><BR>94, v6 Auto. 144,220 miles (engine rebuilt @ 139,330)<P>Mods:<BR>K&N, Duals w/flowmasters, Mac 1 5/8 headers<BR>Crank Underdrive Pulley<BR>3.73's<BR>JMS Chip <P>Sounds: <BR>Alpine Head Unit<BR>3 Alpine V12's, 1 JL Audio 500/1<BR>2 JL 12's, 2 Kenwood 6x9's, 2 Kenwood 5x7's, 6 Alpine Tweets||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fformula88||11-26-2001||12:58 PM||||What you should look for all depends on your taste in the styling for the most part.  <P>Fiero's were made from 84-88.  The 84's were all 4 cyl's.  Starting in 85 a 2.8L V6 was available.  The GT's were not available in 84, were notchback style in 85 and fastback from 86-88.  <P>The 88's had an improved suspension, but the downside to it is parts are hard to come by and more expensive due to the low production volume in 88.  However, 88 V6's, especially GT's but also Formula's tend to be the most pricey.<P>If you want sporty and not too expensive to take care of, go with an 85-87 V6.  Either a GT or an SE.  Then the actual model year you choose would come down to styling differences, since they are nearly identical mechanically.<P>Just be patient, look for bargains, and try to get one without too many miles.  They are old cars and could potentially have a lot of problems.  If it looks well maintained and jhas not been driven into the ground, your probably going to have a more reliable car.<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/formulasig.JPG">         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000002||lou||11-26-2001||01:04 PM||||88gt stick shift for SPEED or AUTO FOR COMFORT .||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pyric||11-26-2001||01:26 PM||||Price wise you don't have too much to worry about.  The blue book value on most fieros is between $1000 and $2000 regardless of condition.  However, in 2004 they will officially start to become a collectors item.  (usually a car has to be 20 years old to be one.)  <P>After that the worth of the car depends on the market.   Considering so few were made, they will probably be pretty quick to reclaim their worth.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>But like you said, you don't want to be ripped off, so unless it is in Perfect condition... I wouldn't pay more than 3000.||||reg||1
